1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:06,000
Model selection is where your quality, your response times, and your bill all get decided at once.

2
00:00:06,000 --> 00:00:11,000
Get the balance wrong and you either overpay or ship something that isn't good enough.

3
00:00:11,000 --> 00:00:17,000
This lesson digs into the three-way trade-off and how to work it. Let's dig in.

4
00:00:17,000 --> 00:00:22,000
Every model decision balances three factors, so let's name them precisely.

5
00:00:22,000 --> 00:00:29,000
Quality is the capability of the model, its accuracy and depth on whatever reasoning, coding, or

6
00:00:29,000 --> 00:00:30,000
analysis the task demands.

7
00:00:31,000 --> 00:00:37,000
Latency is how long the model takes to respond, and it matters most for real-time and interactive

8
00:00:37,000 --> 00:00:39,000
workloads where a person is waiting.

9
00:00:40,000 --> 00:00:45,000
Cost is the per-token price you pay, and the thing to remember is that it gets multiplied across

10
00:00:45,000 --> 00:00:49,000
every single call once your system is running at production volume.

11
00:00:50,000 --> 00:00:56,000
Quality, latency, cost. The reason model selection is hard is that these three usually pull against

12
00:00:56,000 --> 00:01:00,000
each other, so improving one often means giving ground on another.

13
00:01:01,000 --> 00:01:07,000
Picture these three as a triangle. You can't fully max out. You rarely get top quality, top speed,

14
00:01:07,000 --> 00:01:10,000
and the lowest cost all in the same model.

15
00:01:11,000 --> 00:01:17,000
The pattern is consistent. Higher capability usually comes with higher cost and more latency, too,

16
00:01:17,000 --> 00:01:20,000
because a more capable model is doing more work under the hood.

17
00:01:21,000 --> 00:01:27,000
The lighter, cheaper tiers are the ones that respond fastest, so instead of chasing all three, you

18
00:01:27,000 --> 00:01:30,000
figure out which corner of the triangle matters for this job.

19
00:01:31,000 --> 00:01:37,000
Is it a hard problem where quality dominates? A real-time feature where latency rules? A high-volume

20
00:01:37,000 --> 00:01:39,000
batch where cost is everything?

21
00:01:40,000 --> 00:01:46,000
Optimize for the constraint that matters most, and when latency is the binding constraint, the speed

22
00:01:46,000 --> 00:01:51,000
ranking you saw in the tiers lesson tells you which tiers are even in the running and which aren't.

23
00:01:52,000 --> 00:01:58,000
Let's put quality and speed head-to-head because they trade off directly. The higher tiers, Opus and

24
00:01:58,000 --> 00:02:02,000
Fable, deliver the top quality for genuinely hard problems,

25
00:02:03,000 --> 00:02:09,000
but they run at moderate to slower latency because they spend more effort reasoning before they

26
00:02:09,000 --> 00:02:11,000
answer, and that thinking takes real time.

27
00:02:12,000 --> 00:02:19,000
The lighter tiers flip it. Haiku and Sonnet respond fastest, with Haiku in the lead, and they trade

28
00:02:19,000 --> 00:02:22,000
away some capability ceiling to get there.

29
00:02:23,000 --> 00:02:29,000
That's not a flaw. It's the design. For a real-time feature, a fast answer that's good enough beats

30
00:02:29,000 --> 00:02:31,000
a slower answer that's slightly better.

31
00:02:32,000 --> 00:02:37,000
For a hard analysis, you'd happily wait for the stronger result.

32
00:02:37,000 --> 00:02:43,000
Now cost. You saw this ladder in the tiers lesson, so I'll keep it short. Treat Haiku as your

33
00:02:43,000 --> 00:02:48,000
baseline. It has the lowest per-token price, and compare everything else against it.

34
00:02:49,000 --> 00:02:57,000
Sonnet runs a few times Haiku's input rate, and Opus several times, the price of top-tier reasoning and coding.

35
00:02:57,000 --> 00:03:02,000
Those multiples feel abstract until you multiply them by production volume.

36
00:03:02,000 --> 00:03:07,000
At a million calls a day, a few times the price stops being a rounding error and becomes a real line

37
00:03:07,000 --> 00:03:08,000
item in your budget.

38
00:03:09,000 --> 00:03:15,000
The point I want to add is the output side. Output tokens bill higher than input on every tier, so a

39
00:03:15,000 --> 00:03:19,000
model that rambles costs more than one that answers tersely.

40
00:03:20,000 --> 00:03:27,000
Set a sensible max tokens cap, prompt for concise answers, and you save real money at scale.

41
00:03:27,000 --> 00:03:33,000
Here's a lever people forget, and it can save you from switching models at all.

42
00:03:33,000 --> 00:03:38,000
Effort controls how much work the model spends on a given response, and you can turn it up or down

43
00:03:38,000 --> 00:03:40,000
without changing which model you're on.

44
00:03:41,000 --> 00:03:48,000
This matters because tuning effort is often a better first move than jumping to a heavier, pricier tier.

45
00:03:48,000 --> 00:03:55,000
If Sonnet is close, but a little shallow on a task, raising its effort might get you there without paying for Opus.

46
00:03:55,000 --> 00:04:01,000
It cuts the other way, too. Lower effort means less latency and lower cost.

47
00:04:01,000 --> 00:04:06,000
Higher effort means more reasoning depth, all on the same model.

48
00:04:06,000 --> 00:04:13,000
So before reaching for a bigger tier, ask whether adjusting effort on the one you have does the job.

49
00:04:14,000 --> 00:04:18,000
Let's tie the whole thing together with the rule that should drive your final pick.

50
00:04:18,000 --> 00:04:23,000
The best model isn't the strongest one available, and it isn't the cheapest one either.

51
00:04:23,000 --> 00:04:27,000
It's the tier that satisfies every stated constraint at the same time.

52
00:04:27,000 --> 00:04:33,000
So you weigh quality, latency, and cost as a set, not one at a time.

53
00:04:33,000 --> 00:04:38,000
A tier that nails quality but blows your latency budget is out.

54
00:04:38,000 --> 00:04:42,000
A tier that's cheap but can't hit the quality bar is out.

55
00:04:42,000 --> 00:04:47,000
Your discipline is to read all the constraints in the scenario first, and only then choose the model

56
00:04:47,000 --> 00:04:49,000
that clears every one of them together.

57
00:04:50,000 --> 00:04:54,000
That's what separates a considered choice from a favorite model habit.

58
00:04:54,000 --> 00:05:00,000
Let me close by naming the traps that catch people, because knowing them is half the defense.

59
00:05:00,000 --> 00:05:05,000
The first is picking by prestige, always choosing Opus because it's the most capable, while ignoring

60
00:05:05,000 --> 00:05:08,000
that most tasks simply don't need that much model.

61
00:05:09,000 --> 00:05:14,000
The second is picking by price, always defaulting to Haiku to save money, even when the task clearly

62
00:05:14,000 --> 00:05:16,000
needs more capability than Haiku can give.

63
00:05:17,000 --> 00:05:20,000
The third is quieter, ignoring latency.

64
00:05:20,000 --> 00:05:27,000
You optimize hard for quality or cost, and then you miss a real-time budget the workload actually had.

65
00:05:27,000 --> 00:05:32,000
Each trap comes from fixating on one factor. The fix is always the same.

66
00:05:32,000 --> 00:05:36,000
Weigh all three together against the constraints really in the scenario.

67
00:05:36,000 --> 00:05:38,000
Let's frame the big picture.

68
00:05:38,000 --> 00:05:42,000
Model selection balances quality, latency, and cost.

69
00:05:42,000 --> 00:05:46,000
Three forces that pull against each other across the tiers.

70
00:05:46,000 --> 00:05:52,000
It matters because the right pick is the lightest tier that satisfies every constraint at once.

71
00:05:52,000 --> 00:05:56,000
Just remember, tune effort before you switch to a heavier model.

72
00:05:56,000 --> 00:06:01,000
That wraps up quality, latency, and cost tradeoff. Join me in the next one.

